WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.669
-Our next guest
is a Grammy Award-winning

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.071
and Tony- and Emmy-nominated
actress you know from her work

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.272
on "Orange is the New Black"

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.907
and "The Color Purple"
on Broadway.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:11.677
She stars in "The Piano Lesson,"
which is playing on Broadway now

00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:12.912
at the Ethel Barrymore Theatre.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.113
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:16.149
our friend Danielle Brooks,
everybody.

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:17.316
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:27.326 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:35.467 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.704
Whoo!
Welcome back, Danielle!

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.605
How are you?
-I'm so good.

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.674
This sistah over here
playing the drums.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.009
-Knows what she's doing, right?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:46.912
-How cool.
Go ahead, girl.

00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:48.981
-Now, a lot has happened
since the last time

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.783
we were lucky enough
to have you here.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.384
You had a baby girl.

00:00:52.384 --> 00:00:54.687
-I am a mother.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.623
-You got married.
-I's married now.

00:00:57.623 --> 00:00:59.726
[ Cheers and applause ]
Yes.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.794
-Last time we were here,
we were talking about

00:01:01.794 --> 00:01:03.229
potential names
for your daughter.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.598
What did you land on?
-Freeya.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.533
-Oh, that's beautiful.
-She's free.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.835
That's what I want her to know.
-Freeya is beautiful,

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.437
because a lot of the ones
you were suggesting

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:11.905
three years ago were real bad.
-Oh, God.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:12.905
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:14.040
Tell me
you don't have that list.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.508
-No, we got rid of that list.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.810
We're like, "This can't even
be written down on paper."

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.146
So that's wonderful.
Freeya, that's a beautiful name.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.581
-Yes, thank you.
-She's 3 now?

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:24.083
-She's 3 now.
-Does she have any awareness

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.119
of what her mom
does for a living?

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:28.487
-I don't think she does.
She's starting to.

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:29.889
-Yeah.
-Like, I'm on a show called

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:32.825
"Karma's World," too, where
I play the mom on that show.

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:35.361
And so every now and then,
she's like, "Mommy, mommy?

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:37.730
That's my mommy?"
I tried to get her

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.698
in "Color Purple," the movie.
-Okay.

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.734
-That was an epic fail.
-Okay.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:46.306
-Because it was right
smack dab during her naptime.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.441
-Uh-huh.
-And so it's like a scene

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.075
with Fantasia and Ciara.

00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:52.512
And all she's supposed to do
is go to Fantasia,

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:54.047
and she was like,
"No, don't touch me!"

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:55.714
[ Laughter ]
"Don't touch me!"

00:01:55.714 --> 00:01:58.952
So I don't know if she has
a career in acting.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.053
-That's okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:01.287
-I don't think
it's her calling in life.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.455
-That's all right.
It's probably too early to tell,

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.290
but that did seem pretty bad
that she did that.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.426
-Oh, yeah.
-So, this is crazy.

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:11.064
This is a play -- a wonderful
August Wilson play --

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:14.433
and you actually
did a monologue from this play

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.836
when you auditioned
for Juilliard.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:19.037
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
I did a monologue

00:02:19.037 --> 00:02:20.773
of the character Bernice,

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:24.310
and I ended up getting into
all of the colleges.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.414
But I ended up going on with
Juilliard with this monologue,

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.049
and it changed my life.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.483
I'm telling you,
it changed my life because,

00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:34.087
at the time, like,
my grandmother passed away,

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.589
my godmother passed away,
and so -- it just --

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.858
I found this play
at the right time.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:42.895
And now this play has found me,
however many years later,

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.964
and I get to star it in
with Sam Jackson --

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:46.699
Samuel L. Jackson.
-Yeah.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:47.967
-And John David Washington,

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.436
and LaTanya Richardson Jackson
is directing it.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.673
It's like,
♪ "What is going on?" ♪

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:57.009
Just onstage with Sam Jackson
every night.

00:02:57.009 --> 00:03:00.279
-And now, LaTanya Richardson
is an incredibly

00:03:00.279 --> 00:03:04.183
accomplished director, but she's
also Sam Jackson's wife.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:05.718
-Yes.
-Now, I'm wondering,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.220
because I'm always fascinated
by spouses who work together.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:09.989
-Oh, Lord.
-Directors gives notes.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.191
-[ Laughs ]
-Like, directors,

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:13.392
one of their jobs is to give
performance notes.

00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:15.895
Does she do that to Sam
in front of others?

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.997
-She tried in the beginning.
-Uh-huh.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.933
-I think
she chose another angle.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.901
Now that I'm married,
I understand.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:25.037
She said, "You know what?
I'm just gonna take this home."

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:28.074
And I think she tells him
notes and things at home.

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:30.476
-Yeah.
-Because it just --

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.078
it just wasn't working.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:33.980
But they have a really
great relationship.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.515
It's so beautiful.
Shout-out to them

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.616
for being together
for over 40 years.

00:03:37.616 --> 00:03:38.851
-Yeah, that's amazing.
-They've been married

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.486
for so long.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:43.389
Showing us what Black love is
and can be.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.958
-I mean, I'm both impressed,
with 40 years being married

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:47.794
is one thing,
but 40 years being married

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:48.994
and then wanting
to go to work together.

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:51.431
-I know.
40-plus years.

00:03:51.431 --> 00:03:52.831
-Yeah.
-That's crazy.

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:55.268
I ain't even 40 yet.
No shade to the 40 people,

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:57.570
but I'm like, "Wow!"
-Yeah, me neither.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.340
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.974
-What do they say?
Black don't crack.

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:04.410
What do they say for y'all?
-It's all falling to pieces.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.346
[ Laughter ]

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:11.450
-I can't help you there.
Cocoa butter, cocoa butter.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:12.651
-Yeah, there you go.

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:15.154
You're doing worse than helping.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.858
Um, I want to ask you
about this, as well.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:22.261
The play -- one of the themes of
this play is, you know, ghosts.

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.230
-Yes.
Do you believe in ghosts?

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.465
-I don't. Do you?
-No, not really.

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.467
-Okay.
But I do believe in spirits.

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:29.769
Now, I don't know --

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.571
-Now, that seems like
the same to me.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:32.838
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:35.574
-One would say so.
-Yeah, so sell me how you

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.676
perceive the difference
between ghosts and spirits?

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:39.578
-I-I haven't really
come up with a definition.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:41.547
-Okay.
-But ghosts, to me, seems like,

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:45.618
"Ooh, it's a ghost."
Like, it's just B.S., really.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.788
-Yeah.
-But spirits, that's like,

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:51.057
when somebody dies,
their spirit --

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:53.692
I believe their spirit
is still with us.

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:55.460
-Gotcha.
-You know, like, I think about

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:58.630
when you go to a funeral,
and after that funeral, like,

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.267
you still can feel them,
or you, like, sense them

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.436
in a butterfly
or something cheesy.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:06.171
Might seem cheesy, but I really
do believe that, like,

00:05:06.171 --> 00:05:08.808
when we move on
to the next life,

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:11.110
our spirits are still here.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.979
-Now, is part of it that it's
the rest of us that are

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:17.349
keeping your spirit alive
by thinking about you?

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:20.086
-I agree, I agree.
And that's why I think

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.787
storytelling is so important

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:25.290
because that's how
we keep each other alive.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.593
You know, those memories
that we share with one another,

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:31.063
that we do through everything,
whether that's telling a story

00:05:31.063 --> 00:05:34.800
to a baby when they're,
you know, young and 3 years old,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.669
and you're telling a story,
or you're getting married,

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.805
and the brother-in-law
is telling the story.

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:40.840
This is how we transfer
that energy

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.542
and keep each other's
spirit alive.

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:46.079
I mean -- Yeah, I could go on
and on about this but --

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:48.180
-Now, what about this
as a distinction?

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.985
The difference would be,
a ghost tries to talk to you.

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:54.887
Like, a spirit's
just in the world,

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:56.456
and you need to, like,
pull your own message,

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:59.859
but a ghost is like,
"Get out of my house!"

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:02.661
-I think that one might be --
Well, it's crazy

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:04.530
'cause the other day,
I think I saw one.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.433
I know I'm all over the place.
-You are all over the place.

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:09.302
-I know it.
-Where did you see a ghost?

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:11.370
-We just put the nanny cams up.
-Uh-huh.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.106
-And the light --
you know, the light

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:14.740
starts doing different things.
-Yeah.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.676
-And I'm like, "I think I saw
something go across the room."

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:18.777
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:20.413
-But I don't know.
-You know what?

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:21.780
You got something worse
than a ghost.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.649
You have moths.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.917
-That --
-You for real have moths,

00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:27.887
'cause I once said to my wife
when I was looking at

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:28.954
the Nest camera, "[Bleep],"

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:30.055
I was like,
"I think we got a ghost,"

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:31.323
and she was like,
"We got [bleep] moths."

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:33.225
-Moths. I think you're right.
Okay, yes.

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:34.860
So my theory is still the same.
-So you're good.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.329
But here's the thing.
I believe that every moth

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:40.366
is the spirit of a dead person.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:41.600
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, what?

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.935
I'm not allowed
to be all over the place?

00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:44.303
Just you?
-No. Go ahead.

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:46.472
Live your life.
Go ahead, live your life.

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.975
-I think that when we die,
two things happen.

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:50.943
We become moths.
-Moths, okay.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.146
-And we want to eat sweaters.

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:54.847
-That's what
keeps our spirit alive.

00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:57.015
-Because our whole life as
people -- I don't know

00:06:57.015 --> 00:06:58.417
if you feel this,
as well, right?

00:06:58.417 --> 00:06:59.885
I want to eat
a sweater every day,

00:06:59.885 --> 00:07:01.821
but I know as a human person --
-Every day.

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:03.388
-As a human person,
that would be frowned upon.

00:07:03.388 --> 00:07:05.891
-I get an urge every day, Seth.

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:07.359
-You know how delicious
your sweater looks?

00:07:07.359 --> 00:07:10.362
-I get an urge every day, Seth.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:10.362 --> 00:07:12.598
-You get away from me!
[ Laughter ]

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.834
Hey, we were talking backstage
about how wonderful

00:07:14.834 --> 00:07:16.368
the first season
of "Peacemaker" was.

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:18.237
-Oh!
-And just a fantastic --

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.840
You know, I love DC comics.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.942
And he was not
a well-known character,

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:25.011
and James Gunn
did an incredible job.

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:26.078
Did you enjoy your time
making that?

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:28.347
-I freakin' loved
my time making that.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:31.684
James Gunn is that dude.
I loved working with him.

00:07:31.684 --> 00:07:35.387
I can't wait to see what he does
with DC Universe.

00:07:35.387 --> 00:07:37.189
I think he's gonna do
an incredible job,

00:07:37.189 --> 00:07:39.925
and I'm super excited that
I'm still hanging in there...

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.226
-Yeah.
-...for season two.

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:42.795
-Yeah, if you can stay alive
in a superhero movie,

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:43.995
you're doing a good job.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:07:43.995 --> 00:07:45.832
I made it in life.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:49.101
-More importantly, you got your
first wedding anniversary.

00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:50.669
This weekend?
-Yes.

00:07:50.669 --> 00:07:52.338
-So what are you gonna do?
Do you have plans,

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:54.306
or is it sort of
incumbent upon him?

00:07:54.306 --> 00:07:57.175
-I'm going to be secretive
so I don't spill it.

00:07:57.175 --> 00:07:58.510
-All right.
-Because this is the first year.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.480
I just -- I have plans.
But I don't want him to know

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:03.682
'cause he gonna be watching.
-Yeah, oh.

00:08:03.682 --> 00:08:04.883
-Yeah.
-So you're sort of popping

00:08:04.883 --> 00:08:07.653
more surprises on him?
-That's just my vibe.

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.021
-Yeah.
-What about you?

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:10.723
Your first anniversary,
what did you do?

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:11.823
-Uh, I have no memory.
-Work?

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:13.426
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.828
Literally no memory of it, yeah.

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:17.096
-Dang, yo.
Yeah, that's how life is.

00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:19.532
-No, because every day
is a celebration.

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:21.600
[ Laughter ]

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:25.437
-He loves you.
[ Applause ]

00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:29.342
-You guys, this is the
always lovely Danielle Brooks.

00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:31.210
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.944
-"The Piano Lesson"
is playing now

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:35.982
at the Ethel Barrymore Theatre
through January 29th.

00:08:35.982 --> 00:08:38.550
For tickets, please visit
pianolessonplay.com.

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:40.052
we'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

